1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:03,060
In this lecture, we're
going to get into

2
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:05,550
little more detail about
sleeping in the kernel.

3
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:07,050
We're going to understand rules

4
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:08,160
for sleeping in the kernel,

5
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:10,980
and some kernel sleep
wake-up examples

6
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:13,840
from Linux device
driver source code.

7
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:16,640
To get into the reason why we

8
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:18,030
think about sleeping
to begin with,

9
00:00:18,030 --> 00:00:20,060
a lot of times from a
driver perspective,

10
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:21,965
we're talking about blocking IO.

11
00:00:21,965 --> 00:00:25,415
What happens when there's
a request from user space

12
00:00:25,415 --> 00:00:30,045
for something that the
driver can't provide yet,

13
00:00:30,045 --> 00:00:33,230
and you're configured
for blocking IO?

14
00:00:33,230 --> 00:00:36,680
Well, the default case would
be the blocker process and

15
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:40,600
sleep inside the kernel until
that request can proceed.

16
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:42,290
What does that mean when

17
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:43,860
the process is going
to be sleeping?

18
00:00:43,860 --> 00:00:45,230
It means that the scheduler is

19
00:00:45,230 --> 00:00:46,850
going to remove it
from the run queue,

20
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:50,230
and something else is
going to run in its place.

21
00:00:50,230 --> 00:00:52,700
Then you're going to be
waiting for some event to

22
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:56,160
change state before the
process runs again.

23
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:58,640
There's a few rules that we've

24
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:01,335
already discussed a little bit

25
00:01:01,335 --> 00:01:03,090
from the perspective of locking,

26
00:01:03,090 --> 00:01:04,940
but it's good to probably
review those here

27
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:07,355
again now when we're
talking about sleeping.

28
00:01:07,355 --> 00:01:10,085
Never sleep when we're
holding a spinlock,

29
00:01:10,085 --> 00:01:11,840
a seqlock, or RCU lock,

30
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:15,160
which were the different
locks that all use

31
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:18,735
the essentially
spinning or CPU spins

32
00:01:18,735 --> 00:01:22,170
tight loops to obtain processor,

33
00:01:22,170 --> 00:01:24,645
or to obtain specific resources.

34
00:01:24,645 --> 00:01:26,450
Why not do that? Because
they're consuming

35
00:01:26,450 --> 00:01:27,740
the processor while
they're waiting

36
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:30,000
for those events to happen.

37
00:01:30,110 --> 00:01:33,950
If you have multiple processes
that are waiting for

38
00:01:33,950 --> 00:01:37,400
some event to happen and
they're using up all the CPU,

39
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:41,040
it could be a recipe for
deadlocking the process.

40
00:01:42,410 --> 00:01:45,740
Also never sleep if you've
disabled interrupts,

41
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:47,450
and that's probably
pretty obvious.

42
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:50,270
You wouldn't want
interrupt latency to take

43
00:01:50,270 --> 00:01:51,770
the kind of hit that you'd
have if you tried to

44
00:01:51,770 --> 00:01:53,605
do sleeps during interrupts.

45
00:01:53,605 --> 00:01:56,690
There's lots of code associated
with sleep that assumes

46
00:01:56,690 --> 00:01:58,130
that you're never
going to be calling

47
00:01:58,130 --> 00:02:00,630
it from interrupt context.

48
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:03,350
We also want to avoid sleep or

49
00:02:03,350 --> 00:02:05,390
keep the sleep duration

50
00:02:05,390 --> 00:02:07,205
short when holding a
semaphore or mutex.

51
00:02:07,205 --> 00:02:09,245
Remember when we talked about

52
00:02:09,245 --> 00:02:11,780
sleeping in the
context of locking,

53
00:02:11,780 --> 00:02:14,615
but sometimes you just
need to do operations,

54
00:02:14,615 --> 00:02:16,595
like malloc is one example,

55
00:02:16,595 --> 00:02:20,510
lot of times you just have to
do that when you're locked.

56
00:02:20,510 --> 00:02:22,880
If that's the case, you just

57
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:24,920
definitely need to be using
a semaphore or mutex there,

58
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:26,240
and you want to think
about how to keep

59
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,285
those durations as
short as possible.

60
00:02:28,285 --> 00:02:30,290
Lot of times I'll see students

61
00:02:30,290 --> 00:02:32,030
that just know that
they need to lock,

62
00:02:32,030 --> 00:02:33,770
and so they'll
just put a lock at

63
00:02:33,770 --> 00:02:36,145
the beginning and unlock at
the end of the function.

64
00:02:36,145 --> 00:02:38,360
You really want to be a
little bit more careful

65
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:41,875
about knowing when you need
to lock and for what reason.

66
00:02:41,875 --> 00:02:44,240
It gets back to what
we've talked about before

67
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:46,955
about locking data
and not locking code.

68
00:02:46,955 --> 00:02:49,355
Don't just lock a function
from start to end,

69
00:02:49,355 --> 00:02:51,385
know what data you're trying to

70
00:02:51,385 --> 00:02:55,530
synchronize and lock just
around access to that data.

71
00:02:55,530 --> 00:02:59,390
You want to consider
whether you could introduce

72
00:02:59,390 --> 00:03:04,470
a deadlock with any sleep
that you're adding.

73
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:09,935
Really, the difference,

74
00:03:09,935 --> 00:03:11,930
as we talked about when
we discussed locking,

75
00:03:11,930 --> 00:03:14,090
the reason that it's okay to
sleep when you're holding

76
00:03:14,090 --> 00:03:16,415
a semaphore is because
other threads,

77
00:03:16,415 --> 00:03:17,210
when they're blocked on

78
00:03:17,210 --> 00:03:19,770
a semaphore, aren't
actually sleeping.

79
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:24,920
How do you know
that it's safe to

80
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:27,410
sleep when you're holding
a semaphore or mutex?

81
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:28,940
It's really that you
need to know that

82
00:03:28,940 --> 00:03:31,280
any code that's
attempting to obtain

83
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:33,470
the semaphore won't
prevent whatever

84
00:03:33,470 --> 00:03:34,700
your wake-up condition is

85
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:36,700
for waking your process back up.

86
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:39,470
You might say, well, I'll
review the code now,

87
00:03:39,470 --> 00:03:40,730
and I know that now the way

88
00:03:40,730 --> 00:03:42,740
my driver is used,
this won't happen.

89
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:44,585
But how do I know that this

90
00:03:44,585 --> 00:03:46,720
might not happen
sometime in the future?

91
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:48,960
The answer is really you don't.

92
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:52,035
You can document
your assumptions,

93
00:03:52,035 --> 00:03:54,980
but this is part of why
the book recommends

94
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:56,900
that avoiding sleep while

95
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:58,880
holding a semaphore is
really the best option here,

96
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:00,050
so that you don't
have to worry about

97
00:04:00,050 --> 00:04:02,845
that possible future scenario.

98
00:04:02,845 --> 00:04:05,555
The other thing that
the book mentions is

99
00:04:05,555 --> 00:04:08,210
about state as
associated with sleep.

100
00:04:08,210 --> 00:04:10,370
You need to assume that

101
00:04:10,370 --> 00:04:12,380
the state might be completely

102
00:04:12,380 --> 00:04:14,210
different when you
come out of sleep,

103
00:04:14,210 --> 00:04:16,790
wake up, than it was
when you entered sleep.

104
00:04:16,790 --> 00:04:19,105
For instance, if you check

105
00:04:19,105 --> 00:04:21,230
the state of a lock
and then you go into

106
00:04:21,230 --> 00:04:25,125
sleep and you come
back out of sleep,

107
00:04:25,125 --> 00:04:27,390
should you assume that
that lock is still free?

108
00:04:27,390 --> 00:04:29,540
No, you shouldn't because
anything could have

109
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,760
happened during the time
that you were sleeping,

110
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:33,650
and so you have to often write

111
00:04:33,650 --> 00:04:36,220
loops that check some state,

112
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:39,560
and maybe if some
state isn't satisfied,

113
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:41,390
but are only
partially satisfied,

114
00:04:41,390 --> 00:04:42,635
and you need to go to sleep,

115
00:04:42,635 --> 00:04:44,045
make sure when you
come back out,

116
00:04:44,045 --> 00:04:45,620
you re-evaluate all state that

117
00:04:45,620 --> 00:04:48,150
you care about
before you proceed.

118
00:04:48,150 --> 00:04:50,950
It might sound obvious,

119
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:52,700
but before you go to sleep,

120
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:55,325
you have to make sure
that some process

121
00:04:55,325 --> 00:04:57,845
interrupt or kernel event is
going to wake you back up,

122
00:04:57,845 --> 00:05:00,085
based on your sleep condition.

123
00:05:00,085 --> 00:05:04,190
A simple sleep example
is to use wait queues,

124
00:05:04,190 --> 00:05:05,540
and these are
discussed in the book

125
00:05:05,540 --> 00:05:08,090
and shown in a couple
of different examples.

126
00:05:08,090 --> 00:05:09,920
One of the things you'll
notice here is that

127
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:11,420
there's interruptible versions,

128
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:14,060
there's interruptible
timeout versions and these,

129
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:15,950
you could probably guess
what we're doing here

130
00:05:15,950 --> 00:05:18,500
based on our previous
discussion with locking,

131
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:21,320
where wait events are going

132
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:24,050
to be interruptible by
a signal, for instance,

133
00:05:24,050 --> 00:05:26,480
Control C. Typically
you'll probably want to

134
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:29,300
use those in your
driver assuming

135
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:38,260
that you can handle signals
from user space like SGID.

136
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:41,660
The first parameter
here is the wait queue.

137
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:43,985
The wait queue is
initialized just like this

138
00:05:43,985 --> 00:05:46,190
with initialization of
the wait queue head

139
00:05:46,190 --> 00:05:47,870
and just the wait
queue structure.

140
00:05:47,870 --> 00:05:50,120
Then the second is
a condition which

141
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:52,505
is an arbitrary Boolean.

142
00:05:52,505 --> 00:05:54,425
In this case not
a Boolean value,

143
00:05:54,425 --> 00:05:56,030
it looks like it's a parameter

144
00:05:56,030 --> 00:05:57,275
that we're passing
to a function,

145
00:05:57,275 --> 00:05:58,520
but it's actually an arbitrary

146
00:05:58,520 --> 00:05:59,900
Boolean operation that's going

147
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:02,960
to determine whether
it's okay to wake up,

148
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:04,850
and the interruptible
versions are just going

149
00:06:04,850 --> 00:06:06,980
to exit on signals.

150
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:09,515
A little bit more about
what I mean about

151
00:06:09,515 --> 00:06:12,365
condition as opposed
to a Boolean variable.

152
00:06:12,365 --> 00:06:14,810
If I look at this
code in sleepy read,

153
00:06:14,810 --> 00:06:20,030
it looks like I want to wait
until flag is not equals 0.

154
00:06:20,030 --> 00:06:24,805
If I think about what this
would do in a function call,

155
00:06:24,805 --> 00:06:28,840
I would evaluate the flag naught

156
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:31,995
equals 0 and pass a
Boolean into the function,

157
00:06:31,995 --> 00:06:35,270
and I'd check that onetime
inside the function,

158
00:06:35,270 --> 00:06:36,950
but I'd have no idea if flag

159
00:06:36,950 --> 00:06:39,200
is going to change
outside the function.

160
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:42,140
You might look at this and
it looks a little strange at

161
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:43,535
first of like how would you wait

162
00:06:43,535 --> 00:06:45,620
on this condition that
you're passing in?

163
00:06:45,620 --> 00:06:47,720
The answer is, it's because

164
00:06:47,720 --> 00:06:50,405
there's actually some macro
magic going on here that

165
00:06:50,405 --> 00:06:52,670
is going to be using this not as

166
00:06:52,670 --> 00:06:55,535
a Boolean value that
gets evaluated,

167
00:06:55,535 --> 00:06:58,010
but that gets passed
into a macro where it's

168
00:06:58,010 --> 00:07:01,715
re-evaluated over and over as
a part of the sleep status.

169
00:07:01,715 --> 00:07:04,790
The way you can see this
and other macro magic

170
00:07:04,790 --> 00:07:05,810
is really by digging into

171
00:07:05,810 --> 00:07:07,865
the source code for
the Linux kernel.

172
00:07:07,865 --> 00:07:11,420
You can learn a lot about how
macros are used and there's

173
00:07:11,420 --> 00:07:13,700
some really innovative
and creative ways for

174
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:16,130
using macros in
Linux source code,

175
00:07:16,130 --> 00:07:17,720
so kind of walking
through this one

176
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:19,355
with wait event interruptible.

177
00:07:19,355 --> 00:07:21,335
You can see the
second parameter here

178
00:07:21,335 --> 00:07:23,360
is a condition in this define

179
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:25,760
macro and that condition
just gets passed

180
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:27,230
in through a couple
of different layers

181
00:07:27,230 --> 00:07:28,775
to different macros.

182
00:07:28,775 --> 00:07:31,490
Ultimately, there's
some logic here in

183
00:07:31,490 --> 00:07:35,315
the wait event macro that
just says if condition break.

184
00:07:35,315 --> 00:07:40,045
This is essentially expanding
into a block of code

185
00:07:40,045 --> 00:07:42,610
that's replacing this if check

186
00:07:42,610 --> 00:07:46,210
with whatever logic
that I put here.

187
00:07:46,210 --> 00:07:51,195
Every time we're awakened
by the scheduler,

188
00:07:51,195 --> 00:07:54,260
this macro is going to recheck
whatever our condition

189
00:07:54,260 --> 00:07:57,900
was and just go directly back
to sleep if it's not met.

190
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:04,010
The process of waking up,

191
00:08:04,010 --> 00:08:05,390
if you are sleeping,

192
00:08:05,390 --> 00:08:08,390
if you're waiting on a
specific wait queue,

193
00:08:08,390 --> 00:08:10,280
there's wake_up and wake_up

194
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:12,230
interruptible
versions that you can

195
00:08:12,230 --> 00:08:14,720
use to actually wake
your process back up.

196
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:15,830
This is what I mentioned before

197
00:08:15,830 --> 00:08:17,795
that if you're waiting
on a wait queue,

198
00:08:17,795 --> 00:08:19,864
you need to make
sure that something

199
00:08:19,864 --> 00:08:21,960
is going to wake you back up.

200
00:08:22,210 --> 00:08:24,860
You also need to
make sure that it's

201
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:27,290
a different process or
a different interrupt.

202
00:08:27,290 --> 00:08:29,540
Because if you're
sleeping in one process

203
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:32,135
and you're expecting that
process to wake you back up,

204
00:08:32,135 --> 00:08:34,370
that's not going to work because
the process is not going

205
00:08:34,370 --> 00:08:37,470
to be running as you're
currently sleeping.

206
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:40,070
You have to make sure
that a different process

207
00:08:40,070 --> 00:08:41,780
or an interrupt
perhaps is going to

208
00:08:41,780 --> 00:08:43,640
call wake_up with
your wait queue

209
00:08:43,640 --> 00:08:45,425
in order to wake your
process back up.

210
00:08:45,425 --> 00:08:47,420
There's two different
versions of this.

211
00:08:47,420 --> 00:08:51,260
One is the version wake_up

212
00:08:51,260 --> 00:08:52,370
here that's going to call all

213
00:08:52,370 --> 00:08:54,050
processes that are
waiting on the queue,

214
00:08:54,050 --> 00:08:55,610
and the second would only call

215
00:08:55,610 --> 00:08:57,410
interruptible processes
on the wait queue,

216
00:08:57,410 --> 00:08:58,580
so the ones that are using

217
00:08:58,580 --> 00:09:01,790
the wait interruptible version.

218
00:09:01,790 --> 00:09:03,980
You can call these
when you know anybody

219
00:09:03,980 --> 00:09:06,750
that's waiting on your
queue needs to wake up.

220
00:09:07,030 --> 00:09:09,620
Thinking about the example
that we talked about with

221
00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:11,450
blocking IO and there's

222
00:09:11,450 --> 00:09:15,395
a scull_pipe example that
shows this in practice.

223
00:09:15,395 --> 00:09:17,660
When would you want to use
the wake_up functions?

224
00:09:17,660 --> 00:09:20,750
When would you want to
signal that the driver

225
00:09:20,750 --> 00:09:22,115
should wake up from sleep

226
00:09:22,115 --> 00:09:24,500
if it was blocked
on read or write?

227
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:26,795
You'd want to wake_up
a reader queue

228
00:09:26,795 --> 00:09:27,950
so someone that was blocked on

229
00:09:27,950 --> 00:09:32,080
a read if new data was available
because a write came in.

230
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:34,450
Part of your write function
in your kernel should

231
00:09:34,450 --> 00:09:36,670
be to call wake_up on
the wait queue for read,

232
00:09:36,670 --> 00:09:38,980
that way any readers
that were stalled

233
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:42,315
on that wait queue would
get woken up automatically.

234
00:09:42,315 --> 00:09:46,190
Similarly, if you

235
00:09:46,190 --> 00:09:49,250
are handling a case where
the write queue is full,

236
00:09:49,250 --> 00:09:51,755
you're out of buffer
in the write queue,

237
00:09:51,755 --> 00:09:54,005
and the write queue is blocked,

238
00:09:54,005 --> 00:09:57,410
then you'd want to
call wake_up on

239
00:09:57,410 --> 00:09:59,390
the writer queue head

240
00:09:59,390 --> 00:10:03,120
when new space was available
because a read happened.

241
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:06,005
If you handle a read

242
00:10:06,005 --> 00:10:07,400
and you pull some data
out of the buffer

243
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:10,160
and you free more
data for new writes,

244
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:11,330
you can call wake_up on that

245
00:10:11,330 --> 00:10:12,830
writer queue and any writer that

246
00:10:12,830 --> 00:10:14,240
stalled is going to be able to

247
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:17,100
continue and write
into the buffer.